        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">How to read the data</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Hook rate</strong> tells you if the opening works. If hook rate is below 25%, the first 3 seconds need to be rewritten &mdash; the rest of the ad is irrelevant because no one is watching it. Test new hooks against the same body content.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>CTR</strong> tells you if the ad generates enough interest to drive a click. A high hook rate but low CTR usually means the body or CTA is weak &mdash; the viewer watches but doesn't act.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>CPA</strong> is the ultimate metric. A UGC ad with a mediocre hook rate but excellent CPA is still a winner. Don't optimize for vanity metrics &mdash; optimize for cost per acquisition.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Budget: how to start</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Start with $1K&ndash;$5K per month in ad spend for testing. This is separate from production costs ($150&ndash;$500 per video). Commission 10&ndash;20 UGC ad variants per month, test all of them with small budgets ($50&ndash;$100 per variant), kill losers after 48&ndash;72 hours, and scale winners with remaining budget. The goal is finding your winning creative formula before scaling spend.</p>
